@section 2.2 | SDGs in India

@q 2:1-6

@q 2:7-8

@table
SDG India Index (NITI Aayog) | Highlights
First edition | **2018**
2019-20 | **Kerala** first; **UP** in "Performer"
2020-21 | Kerala first; Himachal, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Goa, Uttarakhand next
**2023-24** (July 2024) | **Kerala and Uttarakhand** joint first (79); national score 71; **UP** a **Front Runner** (67)
Categories | Aspirant (0-49), Performer (50-64), Front Runner (65-99), Achiever (100)
@end

**NITI Aayog** is the **nodal body** for SDGs; **MoSPI** prepares the **National Indicator Framework**. In UP the **Planning Department** prepares the state's NIF progress report. India holds about **one-sixth (18%)** of humanity, so the world cannot meet the SDGs without India.

@section 2.3 | History of the Idea

@q 2:15-17

@table
Milestone | Year | Idea
Rachel Carson, *Silent Spring* | 1962 | Pesticide damage; start of the environmental movement
**Club of Rome**, ***The Limits to Growth*** | **1972** | Finite resources limit growth
Stockholm Conference | 1972 | First UN environment conference
**Brundtland Report**, ***Our Common Future*** | **1987** | Defined **sustainable development**: meeting present needs without compromising future generations
Rio Earth Summit (Agenda 21) | 1992 | Action plan
UN SDGs | 2015 | 17 goals for 2030
@end

The **Environmental Kuznets Curve** is an **inverted U**: pollution rises with income at first, then falls after a turning point. The Brundtland Report is not *The Limits to Growth*, so (R) is false.

@q 2:18-22

**Neemrana** (Alwar, Rajasthan) was cited as a model of sustainable rural development. **Sustainable tourism** protects ecology and cultural integrity while serving visitors.

@section 2.4 | Human Capital and Inclusive Growth

@q 2:23-26

**Human capital** is people's skills, knowledge and health. Spending on **education, health and training** raises productivity and yields returns in the future; irrigation builds physical capital instead. Schemes: **Skill India** (2015), PM Kaushal Vikas Yojana.

@q 2:27-29

**Inclusive growth** (the theme of the **11th Plan**, 2007-12) spreads benefits to all through agriculture, infrastructure and social services; high GDP growth alone does not ensure it. The Economic Survey **2011-12** first added a chapter on **sustainable development and climate change**.

@trap
**17 SDGs, 169 targets, 2030.** **SDG 1 = poverty, 4 = education, 6 = water, 10 = inequality, 13 = climate, 14 = life below water, 15 = life on land.** **NITI Aayog** is the nodal agency. **Kerala** tops the SDG index (with Uttarakhand in 2023-24). **Limits to Growth = Club of Rome (1972)**; **Brundtland = Our Common Future (1987)**. **EKC = inverted U.**
@end

